+# Building-block guidance, the rows given it (a system's block_library; the author, 8 Nov 2026: "a new type of expert that
+# encourages compositional storage of the functions"): the expert guidance with its library kept as building blocks, so
+# that a task never met might be solved by composing what earlier tasks taught. lib.py holds small, general functions
+# shared by every task's program, each program is a short composition of them, every verdict on a working program is
+# followed by moving what another task could reuse into lib.py and checking every kept program again with lib.py as
+# saved, and a task with no working program starts from lib.py's index. The rest is PROGRAM_LIBRARY_RULES' word for word:
+# the pairs kept and counted as demonstrations, the kept program run first when the task returns, every program checked
+# on every pair before a submission, and a memory naming the task's files. The kind of function lib.py keeps is shown by
+# BLOCK_LIBRARY_EXAMPLES, after the procedure. {programs}: the folder, which only a stateless row wipes.
+BLOCK_LIBRARY_RULES = (
+    "How to work, in every instance (follow this exactly): keep a library in the folder {programs}, built from reusable "
+    "building blocks. {programs}/lib.py holds small, general grid functions that the programs of many tasks can reuse, "
+    "including tasks not yet met, each with a one-line docstring saying what it does, and each built where it can from "
+    "lib.py's own lower-level functions; the examples at the end show the kind. Each task has two files, named for its "
+    "task id: {programs}/<task id>.py, which "
+    "imports from lib.py and defines transform(grid), which takes an input grid as a list of lists of integers and "
+    "returns the output grid, as a short composition of lib.py's functions; and {programs}/<task id>.json, every "
+    "demonstration pair received for the task, as a list of {{\"input\": grid, \"output\": grid}}. Kept pairs count as "
+    "demonstration pairs you have, and reading them is free.\n"
+    "1. When an instance starts, look for the task's memory (step 4) among those you are shown, and if there is none, for "
+    "its files in the folder. If the task has kept pairs, load them; if it has a program, run it on every kept pair, and "
+    "if it reproduces them all, run it on the test input and submit the result before anything else.\n"
+    "2. Otherwise, or if that answer was wrong, first print lib.py's index, the name and docstring of each of its "
+    "functions. Request demonstration pairs as you judge necessary, write or fix transform, trying compositions of "
+    "lib.py's functions before writing new code, and run it on every pair you have, kept and new, until it reproduces "
+    "all of them; then submit its output for the test input. Checking a program on the pairs is free.\n"
+    "3. Submit only grids that your program produced.\n"
+    "4. After the verdict, add every pair you received to the task's .json file. If your program reproduced all the "
+    "pairs, move every part of it that another task could reuse into lib.py, under a general name with its docstring, "
+    "reusing or extending a function lib.py already has (a new parameter with a default) rather than adding a "
+    "near-copy, and save the program, now a short composition of lib.py's functions, to the task's .py file, replacing "
+    "any older version; then run every kept program on its kept pairs with lib.py as saved, and fix whatever the change "
+    "broke. Keep one memory for the task that lasts between instances, named for its task id, saying where its two "
+    "files are and whether its program reproduces every kept pair. Then finish."
+)
+
+# What the building-block guidance shows of the kind of function lib.py keeps, after its procedure (the author, 8 Nov 2026: "if
+# we could actually find some primitives among the 160 in the rearc generators that are not used in any of those ...
+# tasks, then we could even include some examples in context of the kind of primitives that are worth storing"). Six of
+# the Re-ARC DSL's primitives that no rule of the one-user stream's 25 tasks uses (their verifiers in Re-ARC's
+# verifiers.py), written for grids as lists of rows, and the programs of two training tasks in no user's stream, 7468f01a
+# and b91ae062, each a composition of them that reproduces every pair ARC gives the task (continual-arc-training's
+# blocks/examples.py checks all of it). A row and its reset are shown the same examples, so a share saved against the
+# reset is what the stream added to them. Not a format string: shown as written.
+BLOCK_LIBRARY_EXAMPLES = '''Examples of the kind of functions lib.py keeps, and of two other tasks' programs composed from them:
+
+```python
+def compress(grid):
+    """The grid without its frontiers: every row and every column that is one color throughout."""
+    rows = {i for i, row in enumerate(grid) if len(set(row)) == 1}
+    cols = {j for j in range(len(grid[0])) if len({row[j] for row in grid}) == 1}
+    return [[v for j, v in enumerate(row) if j not in cols] for i, row in enumerate(grid) if i not in rows]
+
+def upscale(grid, factor):
+    """Each cell as a factor x factor block of its color."""
+    return [[v for v in row for _ in range(factor)] for row in grid for _ in range(factor)]
+
+def crop(grid, top, left, height, width):
+    """The height x width subgrid whose top-left cell is (top, left)."""
+    return [row[left:left + width] for row in grid[top:top + height]]
+
+def trim(grid):
+    """The grid without its outermost rows and columns."""
+    return [row[1:-1] for row in grid[1:-1]]
+
+def replace(grid, old, new):
+    """The grid with every cell of color old recolored new."""
+    return [[new if v == old else v for v in row] for row in grid]
+
+def hperiod(grid):
+    """The smallest p such that every row repeats itself every p cells: the grid's width if none is smaller."""
+    w = len(grid[0])
+    return next(p for p in range(1, w + 1) if all(row[j] == row[j - p] for row in grid for j in range(p, w)))
+
+# one task's program: the one shape, cut out by dropping every one-color row and column, mirrored left to right
+from lib import compress
+def transform(grid):
+    return [row[::-1] for row in compress(grid)]
+
+# another's: every cell as a block whose side is the number of colors other than the background
+from lib import upscale
+def transform(grid):
+    return upscale(grid, len({v for row in grid for v in row}) - 1)
+```'''
+
+# The ask after each verdict for a row given building-block guidance: the procedure's step 4 for this task's own files.
+# Unlike PROGRAM_LIBRARY_ASK it asks for no single Python call, since moving parts into lib.py is followed by checking
+# every kept program and fixing what broke. {lib}: lib.py's path, named in full once; the rest as PROGRAM_LIBRARY_ASK's.
+BLOCK_LIBRARY_ASK = (
+    "Now save this task's library.{note} Add every demonstration pair you received to {pairs}. If a program of yours "
+    "reproduced every pair, move every part of it that another task could reuse into {lib}, under a general name with a "
+    "one-line docstring, reusing or extending a function lib.py already has rather than adding a near-copy; save to "
+    "{program} the task's transform(grid), now a short composition of lib.py's functions, replacing any older version; "
+    "then run every kept program in the folder on its kept pairs with lib.py as saved, and fix whatever the change "
+    "broke. If no program of yours reproduced them all, leave the task's program and lib.py as they were. Keep the "
+    "task's memory, named {task}, saying where its two files are and whether its program reproduces every kept pair. "
+    "Then reply with "
+    '{{"action": "finish"}}.'
+)
